#include <avr/io.h>
#include <util/delay.h>

int main() {
    // Set up the LED pin as an output
    DDRB |= (1 << PB2); // Set PB2 as an output

    while (1) {
        // Turn the LED on
        PORTB |= (1 << PB2);
        _delay_ms(500); // Wait for 500ms

        // Turn the LED off
        PORTB &= ~(1 << PB2);
        _delay_ms(500); // Wait for 500ms
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This code is written for an AVR microcontroller (such as the ATmega328P) and uses the `avr/io.h` and `util/delay.h` headers.

Here's how the code works:

* `DDRB` is a register that controls the direction of the pins on Port B. By setting the corresponding bit to 1, we are telling the microcontroller to use the pin as an output.
* `PORTB` is a register that controls the level of the pins on Port B. By setting the corresponding bit to 1, we are telling the microcontroller to set the pin high (i.e. turn the LED on). By clearing the corresponding bit, we are telling the microcontroller to set the pin low (i.e. turn the LED off).
* `_delay_ms` is a function that pauses the program for a specified number of milliseconds. In this case, we are using it to create a delay of 500ms between each on/off cycle.

Note that this code assumes that the LED is connected to pin PB2 on Port B of the microcontroller. You may need to modify the code to match the specific pin connection for your LED.

